     9	from arc_utils import get_bg, paste
    10	
    11	
    12	# Design analysis for this combo variant
    13	# --------------------------------------
    14	# concretization:
    15	#   The source task still uses the same visual vocabulary: a shared-color 4x4 frame
    16	#   around each monochrome 2x2 block, with background-colored openings on the frame
    17	#   border. The fusion changes only the beam geometry. We keep only side openings,
    18	#   because once D1 turns motion into orthogonal rays, corner openings would become
    19	#   ambiguous under the D10 "orthogonal to the gap" remapping. A top or bottom gap
    20	#   now emits a horizontal ray; a left or right gap emits a vertical ray. Because a
    21	#   straight tangent ray cannot continue from the border cell without re-entering the
    22	#   same frame, the painted ray starts one cell outside the gap and then runs along the
    23	#   exterior strip adjacent to that side.
    24	# behavior trace:
    25	#   Original behavior on a top gap: the beam exits upward on a diagonal chosen by the
    26	#   opening's relation to the block, and later collisions may bend that beam again.
    27	#   Fused behavior on a top gap at local (0,1): the beam exits to the cell just above
    28	#   the gap, and from there travels left along that outside row instead of upward. A
    29	#   top gap at local (0,2) travels right. Likewise, a left-side gap at local (1,0)
    30	#   exits to the cell just left of the frame and then travels up, while (2,0) travels
    31	#   down. When one horizontal and one vertical ray meet, the crossing cell is cleared
    32	#   to background and both continue straight, producing a blank-centered plus.
    33	#   The visible difference from the source task is therefore twofold: no diagonals, and
    34	#   no "gap side equals travel side" mapping.
    35	# complexity calibration:
    36	#   If every puzzle used only one orthogonal sign per axis, a solver could mistake the
    37	#   rule for a simpler "all top gaps go left" or "all side gaps go outward" heuristic.
    38	#   The generator therefore requires all four travel directions (left/right/up/down) to
    39	#   appear in each instance, plus at least one horizontal/vertical crossing. That keeps
    40	#   the orthogonal remapping observable without reintroducing the source task's heavy
    41	#   collision-state complexity.
    42	# implicit assumption audit:
    43	#   The original solver depended on three assumptions that no longer hold:
    44	#   - beam direction lived in 8-way space and could begin diagonally;
    45	#   - 45-degree and 90-degree collisions created new direction patterns;
    46	#   - truncation/ownership logic was needed because collisions redirected beams.
    47	#   In this combo, direction is always one of four cardinals, determined by the gap's
    48	#   slot along its side rather than by the side's outward normal. Collisions no longer
    49	#   turn beams; they only punch a 1-cell hole at perpendicular crossings. This lets the
    50	#   solver collapse to fixed-direction propagation with blocked crossing cells.
    51	# placement legality:
    52	#   The generator must guarantee that every chosen side gap has a clear orthogonal path
    53	#   to the grid edge, that no two horizontal rays share a row, and that no two vertical
    54	#   rays share a column. Otherwise same-axis overlaps or head-on clashes would create
    55	#   unsupported cases. It must also forbid any ray from running through another seed.
    56	# visual observability:
    57	#   Each instance contains both horizontal and vertical rays, all four signed directions,
    58	#   and at least one crossing. A human comparing input/output can therefore see that top
    59	#   and bottom openings do not fire outward, because the colored marks appear on the
    60	#   outside strip and extend sideways; similarly left/right openings create vertical
    61	#   marks on the adjacent exterior column. The repeated left/right and up/down examples
    62	#   provide enough support to infer the slot-based sign choice.
    63	# visual cleanliness:
    64	#   Frames keep >=2 cells of clearance, crossings happen only on background cells, and
    65	#   same-axis sharing is rejected. Outputs stay as sparse rows/columns with occasional
    66	#   blank centers, rather than dense overlapping traces that would obscure the mapping.
    67	# color inference:
    68	#   Colors remain purely object-local: each ray uses the color of its own 2x2 block.
    69	#   There is no palette-dependent remapping; only geometry changes.
    70	# new solver assumptions checked against generator:
    71	#   - every emitted gap is a structural side gap in one of the eight side slots;
    72	#   - all four ray directions appear at least once per generated puzzle;
    73	#   - every perpendicular crossing lands on background and never on a seed cell;
    74	#   - no same-axis row/column duplication occurs among emitted rays;
    75	#   - every emitted ray adds at least a few visible cells beyond its seed.
    76	
    77	
    78	_SIDE_GAPS = (
    79	    (0, 1), (0, 2),
    80	    (1, 3), (2, 3),
    81	    (3, 2), (3, 1),
    82	    (2, 0), (1, 0),
    83	)
    84	
    85	_BORDER_ORDER = (
    86	    (0, 0), (0, 1), (0, 2), (0, 3),
    87	    (1, 3), (2, 3), (3, 3), (3, 2),
    88	    (3, 1), (3, 0), (2, 0), (1, 0),
    89	)
    90	_BORDER_INDEX = {p: i for i, p in enumerate(_BORDER_ORDER)}
